ax software to  prepare their returns and they do some  of the better programs ask you a series  of questions that walk you through the  various kinds of deductions and  exemptions that you're entitled to so  that makes it a little bit easier but it  is complicated for people especially if  they have several different sources of  income including business income there's  a follow-up question i believe that the  the turtle Revenue Service refers to  complex tax shelters as abusive tax  shelters is that that's a term that's  generally used yet so this question then  fits in with that how much of the  Internal Revenue Service shortfall which  again you have to help me with the  definition is I think that's the  difference between what is owed and what  is collected it's it's the difference  between what should be paid if everybody  paid what they were supposed to under  the law yes okay it's sometimes  popularly referred to as the tax gap so  how much of this shortfall then is a  result of the various interpretations of  what is allowable and what is not  allowable under the various tax shelter  schemes that are available particularly  to high-net-worth individuals well I  don't have I don't I don't know what  that number is I've been at four to use  I don't know but I will say it's one of  the love the total I'll use just a round  number here  are perhaps 300 billion dollars a year  that's that's a conservative number in  the sense that if it's anything it's  higher than that that is the difference  between what should be paid and what is  paid the kind of tax shelters or abusive  shelters you're talking about which are  primarily used by large corporations and  very very wealthy individuals those are  a major percentage of that it's not by  any means all of it but they are  probably the largest single or one of  the largest single elements in that  three hundred billion dollars right okay  tax estimates federal tax estimates in  California I don't know about other  states there's not only a federal tax  estimate but there's a state tax  estimate do you think the federal income  tax estimate payments will ever go the  way of the Model T car well I doubt it  unfortunately I did it because if people  are are not having enough money withheld  from their taxes where they have income  where there is no withholding you know  in order to keep up on a current basis I  think all the tax code that meant most  of the states as well as the federal  government do require people to make  quarterly payments and it is it is a bit  of a burden but that's that's probably  not one of those things that's going to  go away change the subject just for a  second militia groups that think that  they don't have to pay taxes and the  taxes are illegal how proactive is the  IRS in dealing with anti-government and  anti-tax militia groups that don't pay  their taxes and don't feel the  government has the right to collect them  yeah I think that they're generally  called tax protesters and what they are  people that that have a variety of  theories as to why they don't know any  tax all of which are bogus completely  bogus and been overruled by the courts  many many times and some of them are are  pretty funny they come up with all kinds  of theories that you know the sixteenth  Amendment of the Constitution was never  properly ratified because Ohio wasn't  really a state when the men was passed I  mean I can go on and on  things but they are a serious problem  and the IRS does have resources targeted  against those those people that don't  file returns and don't pay but again  it's a resource problem there are quite  a number I don't have a number to give  you but there this is not a handful of  people this is not like 10 or 20  nutcases out there these are these are  large numbers of people spread actually  surprisingly around various parts of the  country and it's quite resource  intensive to go out and investigate  investigate people in those categories  so while I think the IRS has done a  better job than perhaps it used to at at  putting resources to that problem it  comes right back to the issue that there  aren't enough resources to go around so  there are a lot of people that that  pursue that kind of a kind of activity  that are that are still doing it it's  time for another reminder to our radio  audience you're listening to the  Commonwealth Club of California's radio  program and our speaker today is former  commissioner of the Internal Revenue  Service Charles Rosati he's discussing  how to reform the IRS I think I could  describe the next question or two also  with a political background the Clinton  administration has had its share of  scandals many of which were purported to  be pursued from the right wing but did  President Clinton then retaliate by  encouraging the IRS to single out  Clinton opponents to be audited by your  agency this was a claim according to the  questioner that was made by judicial  watch the public interest group that  investigates public corruption that  there have been allegations of that kind  made over the years many many times and  they continued under different  administrations they continued during  during my period in office and as part  of the reforms that go back actually  before the 1998 act but but especially  they were strength of the 19  there are some very very stringent  safeguards against those kinds of  activities as a matter of fact one of  them is that there is an investigative  agency in the treasury department that  has a thousand a staff of a thousand  people it's called the treasury  inspector general for tax administration  and the it's not part of the IRS it's  part of the Treasury Department and the  only mission of that organization of a  thousand people is to investigate the  IRS so you have a hundred thousand  people in the IRS and you have a  thousand people investigating them in  case there's any allegations and they  are very very rigorous in their  investigations if there's any allegation  of any sort by a taxpayer by an employee  by any member of the public they are  followed up on thoroughly during my term  there were many such allegations they  were all followed up on and I can tell  you that that there is no substance to  any of that in fact one of the best  things even before I got there long  before i got there that has happened as  a result of problems going back to the  50s is that over time there have been  more and more these safeguards put in so  that the one problem that we don't have  in this country is politically directed  audits well here's a follow-up about  politically directed audits and it might  be judicial watch as well that said how  about the audits of the National Rifle  Association the National Review fox news  host Bill O'Reilly well I mean I'm not  going to talk about any specific audit  but I can tell you that that the process  of selecting selecting any return or any  individual for what it is based on an  extremely rigorous process and any  attempt by anybody outside the agency to  influence that process would be subject  to so many investigations and would be  reported so quickly that that your head  would spin as a matter of fact there's a  in the 1998 while they put a specific  provision in that basically requires any  employee of the IRS any employee of the  IRS that who believes that there has  been any communication of any sort from  anyone outside the  ress in the executive branch along the  lines of directly an audit that they  have to immediately report it to about  four different places including the  inspector general the Ways and Means  Committee and and a number of other  places so I can I spent five years there  I had never been there before whenever  the way these allegations I reviewed  those reports very very carefully with  the inspector general and others and  without exception they were found to be  baseless now sometimes if an  organization is or a person is audited  that might have a political profile  people will make that allegation but the  fact that they were audited and they  were a high political profile does not  mean that they were selected for an  audit because they had a high political  profile it means they had something that  they needed to be audited about we've  had several questions that I've put them  all together at a very brief response  here on outsourcing can and does the IRS  use outsourcing as a way to save money  should it use it more frequently if it  actually does and as there are  opportunities to do more of it I mean  they're not they're not enormous  opportunities where you can take the  whole agency and outsource it because  most of what the IRS does is is deal  with taxpayers taxes and that's  something that is widely viewed to be  something that needs to be done as a  governmental function but there are  there are a number of things that are  outsourced a lot of the technology  almost all the technology development is  outsourced there are various kinds of  support functions that are being  considered for outsourcing and in fact  there's an initiative throughout the  federal government to look at what's  called competitive sourcing to try to  look at anything that can be competed to  to have that done so that is a tool that  can be used and it is being used at the  conclusion of your term in 2002 how much  more if any management control and  authority did you have compared to 1997  when you first took the job well I think  in terms of legal authority and what's  on on the books the IRS commissioner has  a significant amount authority to run  the agency has no authority on  tax code but he has a significant  whoever's office does have a significant  amount of authority operating within  some rules and constraints but does have  a significant amount of authority what  what is what I think is more important  is because of many of the changes that  we were able to make and that were  facilitated by the 1998 act the internal  structure of the agency and the way that  it was organized and set up made it much  more practical to actually exercise that  authority and especially to exercise it  to do things like change the enforcement  strategy for example oh and just to give  you a simple example in the in the way  that the agency was set up in the part  at the time I got there which was a  legacy of years of changes back through  the 50s it was essentially like having  43 different IRS's because there were  districts and other kinds of units that  each had managers in charge and they  essentially ran things the way that they  way they thought saw fit in those in  those areas so the headquarters could  give out directives but then each each  unit would sort of interpret them  whatever way they wanted to and even on  things like technology everybody was  buying their own different standards of  technology so it was like having a  Museum of Technology each each each  example that had ever been produced over  the previous 40 years existed somewhere  in the IRS that was not an efficient way  to exercise authority or even just to  get things done so as a result of the  reorganization and some other changes  the agency is in a better position to  have a unified strategy and to actually  execute on its plans and it's using that  authority under the current Commissioner  as well as while I was there well mr.  Rosati one of the highest visibility  issues that President Bush is putting  forth is about the reform of Social  Security and can you comment and what  role the IRS would play should there be  additional taxes withheld from employees  paychecks to help fund it what is your  feeling on that I actually don't know I  don't think there's been a purple and  proposed the IRS does collect the fight  guys it's called the social security  taxes it does have it is the agency that  does that so to be  extent that there would be a change in  the rules for for collecting or paying  those taxes it would be the IRS I  presume that would have the job of  administering that but I don't know at  least I'm not aware of that there's been  any details laid out on how that would  work there's been several questions also  on offshore shelters I think one of the  news items that I'm familiar with is the  repatriation aspect by corporations of  bringing money back to this country from  the Cayman Islands and other areas  whether these shelters exist could you  just generally or says specific as you'd  like to be sir talk about offshore  shelters are some of them considered  criminal and if so what makes them  criminal vs. acknowledged and legal  shelters well that is actually a very  good question a very complex question  but just to try to simplify it there's  there's there's some of what was in  there that he was talking about repay  she and it isn't a shelter at all it's  just part of the tax code it just says  that if a u.s. corporation earns income  in a foreign country it does not get  taxed don't have to actually pay the  cash tax on that in most cases in  something in a lot of cases until it  actually brings the cash back that  that's just the way the tax code is set  up and so just talking about  repatriation is not a tax shelter what  does a tax shelter is finding ways to  manipulate the income of an individual  or a business to make it appear that  that income is earned in a someplace  that is a tax shelter that whether it's  the Cayman Island a tax haven where it's  the Cayman Islands or anywhere else so  that it appears that the income is  earned their and therefore the tax is  not paid and as long as the cash isn't  brought back it stays there so that can  be a tax shelter and then third one that  you mentioned what with respect to  criminal issues has to do with something  entirely different it's not involved in  trying to you know subtly shade where  you earn your income it's just hiding it  so for example there was a newspaper  report a couple of weeks ago about a  case that the IRS brought a criminal  case in court against an individual who  was a entrepreneur in the  telecommunications industry who was  accused of hiding  450 million dollars of capital gains  income just flat hiding it you know  offshore places no pretense of shelters  just just putting it there and not and  not paying tax on it that is obviously a  criminal if proven it would be a  criminal a criminal offense i do want to  point out that that made story because  it was 450 million dollars of income it  was hidden and so in that sense that was  an unusual case because there aren't  that many people that have 450 million  dollars to hide but what he was doing  was not unusual there are hundreds of  thousands of people doing the same thing  flat hiding money in you know offshore  places there's a question here about a  book by david johnston I think it's  David Cay Johnston who was the New York  Times reporter I'm familiar with the  book yes I am what is your opinion and  your reaction to this book entitled  perfectly legal well what what what mr.  Johnston has done he's a New York Times  reporter that covers taxes in the IRS is  he's laid out in a you know fairly  thorough fashion a litany of some of the  schemes that people have used to reduce  income except he is focused on his title  perfectly legal I'm things that are at  least color ibly or arguably legal under  the tax code so he's arguing essentially  the case of how manipulable the tax code  currently is which of course is one of  the points I made in my presentation  that because of the complexity that the  tax code it allows for this kind of  manipulation so if you want to have an  exhaustive list of how some of these  things can be done read mr. Johnston  book thank you sir are you concerned  that the results of corporate research  credits are often exported as part of  offshoring oh I don't know I think I'll  duck that when I'm not sure I know the  answer to that after your 5 years at the  Internal Revenue Service would you like  to tackle another government agency  that's an easy one no I did it once  that's that's once in a lifetime this  questioner States and this is kind of  discouraging  if it's true I have no reason to believe  it's not it says many of the changes in  the IRS implemented under your watch  have been reversed please explain your  stance and your feelings about this  taxation without representation as  tyranny well I I just don't agree with  the implication I don't think they've  been reversed i think my successor whose  name is Mark Everson has done an  excellent job of building on what we did  he's taken the reorganized the agency  and some of the new technology and he's  using it he's also doing a very vigorous  job of trying to do what we started  which was the direct the enforcement  resources against the real abusers of  the system as opposed to just giving out  traffic tickets to people with a minor  problem I agree with that entirely so I  don't agree with that implication of  that question well mr. Rosati  unfortunately we've reached the point in  our program where there's time for only  one last question and I'm going to go  back to a category of questioning that  we did at the beginning regarding audits  and it says what's the most common  cheating that people do on their taxes  and I guess they're talking about  individuals well I think I think I've  answered that once before I don't think  I want to give a specific answer the  most common one but generically I mean  there are people to take deductions  they're not entitled to there are people  leave out income that they should be  reporting and there are people that you  know use devices to generate losses to  offset gains that they had which they  really weren't entitled to those are the  kind of things well some of these some  of these losses are are either shelters  or schemes if you will that have been  established maybe by the accountants and  the attorneys that people hire if if  that's the case should there be a  governmental action against the  accountants and the attorneys as opposed  to the taxpayers and there has been that  was one of the things that we started  and my term was to initiate audits of  promoters of tax shelters and that has  been continued vigorously and there been  some very significant cases brought  against promoters some of whom were  accounting firm some of whom were law  firms some of whom were other  kinds of tax promoters our thanks to  Charles Rosati former commissioner the  Internal Revenue Service and author of  the book many unhappy returns for  joining us today and we also thank our  audiences here and on radio and on  television and now this meeting of the  Commonwealth Club of California  celebrating over a hundred years of  enlightened discussion is adjourned mr.  Rosati will sign books right here and if  you have one please line up right in the  center here Charles Rosati served as IRS  commissioner from 1997 to 2002 he's  currently a senior advisor for the  carlyle group in Washington DC many  unhappy returns is published by harvard  business school press on the web at HBS  press dot org
 

